Fix out-of-bounds read in tre_parse_bracket_items on an unterminated
POSIX character class.

A pattern whose character class is cut short at the closing colon, such
as the 9-byte pattern "[[:alpha:", makes the parser read past the end of
the pattern buffer:

    ==1==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow
    READ of size 4
        #0 tre_parse_bracket_items src/extra/tre/tre-parse.c:282
        #1 tre_parse_bracket      src/extra/tre/tre-parse.c:453
        #2 tre_parse              src/extra/tre/tre-parse.c:1388

After scanning forward to the colon that ends the class name, the code
assumes a ']' follows it and skips both with "re = endptr + 2".  When
that colon is the final character of the pattern, re then points one
element past ctx->re_end.  The loop guard tests "re == ctx->re_end",
which an overshoot slips past, and the next iteration dereferences re.

Require the closing colon to actually be followed by ']' before
consuming both -- an unterminated class is then rejected with REG_ECTYPE
as it already is when no colon is found at all -- and relax the loop
guard to catch an overshoot rather than only exact equality.

Reachable from R via grep(), sub(), agrep() and friends whenever the
pattern comes from an untrusted source.

Found by fuzzing R with libFuzzer + AddressSanitizer.

Upstream status: NOT YET REPORTED to the R Bugzilla as of 2026-08-05.
Delete this patch once the fix lands in R trunk (ossfuzz.sh will report
it as already-applied in the meantime, which is harmless).

--- a/src/extra/tre/tre-parse.c
+++ b/src/extra/tre/tre-parse.c
@@ -275,7 +275,7 @@
   while (status == REG_OK)
     {
       skip = 0;
-      if (re == ctx->re_end)
+      if (re >= ctx->re_end)
 	{
 	  status = REG_EBRACK;
 	}
@@ -317,7 +317,7 @@
 	      DPRINT(("tre_parse_bracket:  class: '%.*" STRF "'\n", REST(re)));
 	      while (endptr < ctx->re_end && *endptr != CHAR_COLON)
 		endptr++;
-	      if (endptr != ctx->re_end)
+	      if (endptr + 1 < ctx->re_end && *(endptr + 1) == CHAR_RBRACKET)
 		{
 		  len = MIN(endptr - re - 2, 63);
 #ifdef TRE_WCHAR
